=== layout: one-panel

# Fruit fly problem

@body

- Cross flies heterozygous at st and bw.
- Both recessive alleles affect eye color; the double recessive is white.
- Among 240 offspring: 150 wild type, 36 scarlet, 46 brown, 8 white.

*Do these counts fit independent assortment (9:3:3:1)?*

=== layout: one-panel

# Predict the expected counts

@body

| Phenotype | Observed | Expected |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Wild type | 150 | 135 |
| Scarlet | 36 | 45 |
| Brown | 46 | 45 |
| White | 8 | 15 |

=== layout: one-panel

# Calculate the contributions

@body

| Phenotype | O | E | (O-E)^2^/E |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Wild type | 150 | 135 | 1.667 |
| Scarlet | 36 | 45 | 1.800 |
| Brown | 46 | 45 | 0.022 |
| White | 8 | 15 | 3.267 |

=== layout: one-panel

# Sum and determine df

@body

- &chi;^2^ = 225/135 + 81/45 + 1/45 + 49/15
- &chi;^2^ &asymp; 6.76
- Four categories; no parameters estimated

*df = 4 - 1 = 3*

=== layout: one-panel

# What does the fly result mean?

@body

- &chi;^2^ = 6.76; df = 3
- Use the right-tail probability table.

*Is p above or below 0.05?*

Does this provide evidence against independent assortment?

=== layout: one-panel

# Compare with the critical value

@body

- df = 3; &alpha; = 0.05
- Critical value &asymp; 7.82

*&chi;^2^ = 6.76 < 7.82: fail to reject H~0~.*

The counts do not provide sufficient evidence against the proposed ratio.

=== layout: one-panel

# Fruit fly conclusion

@body

- &chi;^2^ &asymp; 6.76; df = 3; p &asymp; 0.08
- p > 0.05: fail to reject independent assortment.

The data are compatible with 9:3:3:1, but do not prove the model.

=== layout: one-panel

# Which way does p move?

@body

*With df fixed, would a larger &chi;^2^ increase or decrease p?*

=> appear
A larger statistic leaves less area to its right, so *p decreases*.

