=== layout: section

# Are Mendel's numbers too good to be true? Did Mendel cheat?

=== layout: one-panel

# Are Mendel's results unusually close?

@body

- Many observed ratios are very close to 3:1.
- Our chi-square statistics range from about 0.015 to 0.607.
- A large p-value for one experiment is not suspicious by itself.

*Across many experiments, are there fewer large deviations than expected?*

@notes

Paraphrases the source textbook discussion. The next graph compares 69 experiments.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Observed and expected deviations in Mendel's experiments](assets/lect07d-chi_square/image_020.png)

@caption

69 experiments: fewer large deviations than expected.

@notes

Source caption suggests that some experiments with large deviations may have been discarded or repeated. This is a historical interpretation, not proof of deliberate falsification.

=== layout: one-panel

# Bias does not require deliberate fraud

@body

- Mendel was an early pioneer of counting segregating traits.
- Rechecking only surprising results can systematically improve apparent agreement.
- Selective repetition or exclusion can affect the distribution of deviations.

*A statistical pattern alone does not establish deliberate falsification.*

@notes

Paraphrases the source's historical commentary; the original passage remains in the legacy ODP/PDF.

